// result_end
\caption(Actual eight held-out scenes with green truth and red learned refined detections. Red boxes can be missed-class or duplicate predictions; green is drawn above overlapping red outlines for visibility.)
\alttext(Actual eight held-out scenes with green truth and red learned refined detections. Red boxes can be missed-class or duplicate predictions; green is drawn above overlapping red outlines for visibility.)

\image(figures/rcnn_comparison.svg)
\caption(The same class probabilities before and after learned class-specific box decoding. Per-class AP omits categories with no test truth. The comparison makes no assumption that regression must improve every class.)
\alttext(Two panels compare overall AP and per-class AP50 for unrefined and refined candidate boxes.)


> Conclusion

The controlled box fixture checks coding and crop padding; actual galleries show the proposals and warped regions used for fitting. Foreground classification and regression use different overlap cutoffs, and the visible multitask loop selects on separate validation scenes. The held-out comparison uses identical probabilities and changes only box decoding.
On 23 target digits, refinement raises COCO-style AP75 from 0.492616 to
0.505146, leaves inclusive VOC AP50 at 0.682429 and lowers AP50:95 from
0.485728 to 0.428570. The class-specific regressor therefore gives a mixed
localization result in this small run, not a uniform improvement. The actual
gallery retains the extra and merged boxes.

> References

- [Girshick et al. (2014), Rich feature hierarchies for accurate object detection and semantic segmentation](https://arxiv.org/abs/1311.2524).
- [Girshick (2015), Fast R-CNN, multitask loss](https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.08083).
- [Ren et al. (2015), Faster R-CNN box-coordinate parameterization](https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01497).
